The `setInterval` callback is `async` but `setInterval` does not await the returned promise. If `server.waitForRequestsIdle()` rejects—for example, because `bail()` closes the server while the interval is mid-flight—the rejection becomes an unhandled promise rejection that can crash the Node.js process depending on the runtime version. Wrap the body in a `try/catch` or chain a `.catch()` to suppress or log the error gracefully:
```ts
waitForModuleGraph = setInterval(() => {
  void (async () => {
    try {
      if (server.moduleGraph.fileToModulesMap.size > 0) {
        clearInterval(waitForModuleGraph);
        waitForModuleGraph = undefined;
        await server.waitForRequestsIdle();
        watcherChangeHandler?.();
      }
    } catch {
      // server was closed before idle; no-op
    }
  })();
}, 1000);
```
